# Without Mercy or Favour...!
Tamil title: தயவு தாட்சண்யமின்றி...!

An economic crisis has arisen in the country.

Everyone must share in the hardship.

All wasteful expenditure must be prevented.

Economy must be practised in every way.

The Central and Provincial Governments must reduce their expenditure without mercy or favour.

People must use commodities as sparingly as possible.

More than thirty lakh rupees will be spent on the Jaipur Congress Conference. Considering the high prices at which goods are now sold, it cannot be said that even thirty lakhs is excessive.

A silver chariot has been arranged to take the president of the Jaipur Congress Conference in procession. Two special trains from Madras, three from Calcutta and separate trains from several other places are travelling to the Jaipur Conference. Experts in their respective fields have been invited from many regions to stage dances, songs, dramatic entertainments and *kummi* performances in the conference pavilion.

The statements cited at the beginning are some of the important passages in the resolutions sent by the All India Congress Committee to the Jaipur Conference. The statements that follow are some of the arrangements made by that same committee for the conference.

The very committee that sent the All India Congress Conference a resolution declaring that the country was in an economic crisis has arranged to conduct the conference at a cost of thirty to fifty lakh rupees. Those who resolved that everyone must share in the hardship have arranged a silver chariot in which to take their president in procession. Those who passed a resolution that all wasteful spending must be prevented have arranged dances, songs, dramatic entertainments and *kummi* performances in the conference pavilion. Those who say that economy must be observed in every way have arranged numerous special railway carriages for themselves at enormous cost and are travelling to the conference in them.

Friends should compare the gulf, as between a mountain and a hollow, separating the words “economic crisis,” “sharing hardship,” “wasteful expenditure” and “economy” from the words “thirty lakhs in conference expenses,” “silver chariot,” “special trains,” “dance,” “song,” “entertainment” and “kummi.” They should consider whether there is even a mustard seed's worth of agreement between word and deed, and whether people of this kind can remedy the country's hardships, losses, grievances and unrest. We write this so that everyone in general, and Congress comrades in particular, may reflect upon it.

The Congress Working Committee has recognised the country's economic crisis, acquired enough strength to advise that expenditure be reduced accordingly, and gained the power to issue orders to the Government. Now that the white rulers have departed, it need not convene a conference merely for its own sake. It obtained the freedom it demanded and ought to undertake the great task of safeguarding that freedom. We ask it to consider whether conducting a conference costing many lakhs is proper, necessary or useful.

Freedom has been won, and the leaders of the freedom struggle have assumed responsibility for administering it. In these circumstances, why must the Congress organisation, which was founded solely upon attaining freedom and which convened many conferences to instil in the people both a sense of freedom and enthusiasm for winning it, hold another conference now? Earlier, it convened conferences upon the basis of driving out alien British rule. Is the Congress organisation now holding a conference upon the basis of driving out poverty?

The basis of the Congress organisation has now changed. Its purpose is no longer to drive anyone out, but to unite everyone in this country. Has today's Congress conference been convened on that basis? Or has it been convened at least on the basis that, although we struggled for and obtained freedom, several parties in the country refuse to share in that freedom and live within the Indian Union, and that their grievances must therefore be understood and suitable remedies found? There is no room even to say that the conference has been convened for such a purpose.

After prohibitory orders under Section 144 had been imposed upon meetings connected with the Dravidar Kazhagam's anti-Hindi civil disobedience, did not Madras ministers and Congress leaders hurriedly arrange a special train and leave for the Jaipur Conference?

At Kumbakonam, Tiruvarur, Nannilam, Kovilpatti, Tirukkoyilur and other places, the authorities possess enough power to impose prohibitions and take action against the Dravidar Kazhagam. Can we avoid wondering whether the Jaipur Conference will be used to devise plans for further strengthening that authority?

Today's independent system of government is proceeding to rule the entire country through such repressive methods. How can we refrain from telling the public that the Congress organisation has convened its conference for that purpose, wasting lakhs of public money?

We thought the Jaipur Conference would devise plans to reduce wasteful expenditure as resolved by the Congress Working Committee. Yet the very conference it has convened is wasting many lakhs!

[Website Images 4 and 5 contain substantially the same printed passage. It is translated once above; both scans remain intact in the verbatim Tamil source.]

And under these circumstances, we are told that an economic crisis has arisen in the country; that everyone must share in the hardship; that wasteful expenditure must be stopped; and that economy is necessary! What splendid preaching!

Through a resolution, the Congress Working Committee advises the Government that “the Central and Provincial Governments must reduce their expenditure without mercy or favour.” Yet it spends lakhs upon a conference of its own! Let us set aside for a moment the question whether this is just or proper.

In a country described as the summit of destitution and the dwelling place of poverty, the Governor-General's monthly salary is twenty-one thousand rupees, with many more thousands for other overhead expenses.

Ambassadors abroad receive twelve thousand, eight thousand or six thousand rupees, together with two, three or even nine cars for each of them!

The Prime Minister of the Indian Union receives three thousand rupees, apart from allowances.

Provincial ministers receive one thousand five hundred rupees, apart from allowances.

Members of the Legislative Assembly receive one hundred and fifty rupees, apart from allowances.

Did the Congress Working Committee ask why? It orders the Central and Provincial Governments to reduce expenditure without mercy or favour, but it is difficult to understand why it has shown its own mercy and favour without restraint in this matter alone. Had its resolution calling for reductions in government expenditure included even one reference to reducing the expenses listed above, one could believe that it had truly recognised the country's economic crisis and was striving to change that condition.

But the Congress Working Committee did not undertake that work. In other words, it has no sincere desire to relieve the real economic crisis. Recently the Madras ministers submitted a request saying that the salaries and allowances received by ministers and ambassadors were insufficient and should be increased. Because they had to conduct frequent feasts and functions, they said, their present income was inadequate; they therefore asked for another four hundred rupees.

The Congress Working Committee tells the Central and Provincial Governments to reduce expenditure without mercy or favour. Provincial ministers, however, say that their salaries are insufficient because they must give feasts, and ask the Government to raise their salaries without considering mercy or favour! What a course this system of independent government follows!

There is another absurdity. The committee that resolves to reduce expenditure includes ministers who complain that their present salaries are inadequate and ask for still more. While attending the Working Committee, they perceive the country's economic crisis and feel that expenditure must be reduced. Once its proceedings end and they return to their ministerial seats, however, they perceive their own “economic crisis”: they lack enough money for the feasts and functions they conduct, and conclude that the Government must pay them still more.

The country's general economic crisis, visible before their eyes in the Working Committee, disappears when they sit upon the ministerial seat and entertain all who come and go. Their own economic crisis alone then appears before them, confuses their judgement and makes them open their mouths to demand, “Our salaries are insufficient; give us more!”

It is people possessing this kind of political wisdom and this “broad” vision of understanding the country's condition and acting accordingly who declare that economy must be practised in every sphere and that expenditure must be reduced without mercy or favour. What splendid mercy and favour they display!

19 December 1949

---NOTES---
The recurring phrase `தயவு தாட்சண்யமின்றி` literally invokes acting without compassion or partiality; “without mercy or favour” preserves both the official austerity formula and the article's closing irony. `படிச் செலவு` is rendered contextually as an allowance. The scan reads Section 144 in the passage about prohibitory orders; two OCR versions had misread it as 128 and 124. Website Images 4 and 5 substantially duplicate the same printed passage, so both are retained in the Tamil source while the English translation presents the passage once.
